{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85b779383792726d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9015\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: E002 external_dependency scored 0 with a hedged rationale about possible\n10\t    upstream schema work, but the epic scope pins the custom-properties approach that\n11\t    model-registry.md documents as the extension point (midstream-only change); either\n12\t    re-score to 1 or tighten the rationale to state the residual upstream-tracking\n13\t    risk explicitly\n14\terror: null\n15\t---\n16\t## Review Summary\n17\t\n18\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n19\tRecommendation: accept\n20\t\n21\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 4-epic output is expected. No conditional branch files exist, and none are required (no bounded-outcome investigations).\n22\t\n23\t## Criterion Details\n24\t\n25\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n26\tAll four HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (P0) → E001; HLR-2 (P1) → E002 + E003, split along the component/team seam the architecture context dictates (registry backend vs Dashboard-rendered registry UI); HLR-3 (P1) → E001 bundled, with the `(bundled)` flag and inline rationale recorded directly in the HLR Traceability Matrix row — the compliant bundling shape, since the diff view is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface as HLR-1 (priority-label difference on one surface is redundant requirement wording, not a boundary); HLR-4 (P2) → E004. Priority inheritance is clean: E001 (P0) blocks P0 work; E003 (P1) depends only on P0/P1 epics; E004 is docs-authoring and exempt (strategy `priority: Major` → P1, correctly explained in Health Warnings). No priority collapse — the only bundled lower-priority HLR (HLR-3) is same-surface, not a deferrable distinct feature.\n27\t\n28\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n29\tNo circular dependencies. Every frontmatter `dependencies` list matches the summary diagram exactly (E003: [E001, E002]; E004: [E001, E002, E003]; E001/E002: []), and arrows run dependency → dependent per convention. Edges are justified: E001→E003 (the registry entry point opens the comparison surface E001 builds), E002→E003 (E003 consumes the run-lineage reference E002 exposes via the registry REST API — a genuine data dependency), and E001/E002/E003→E004 per Rule 11 (docs blocked by every implementation). E001 ∥ E002 correctly have no edge (different components, teams, repos; no shared artifacts). No konflux-onboarding or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not in play. Critical path length 3 matches the summary and fits the M-strategy heuristic (3-4). Cross-check of epic content found no uncaptured data/artifact dependencies.\n30\t\n31\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n32\tComponent/team tuples are consistent and architecture-grounded: E001/E003 (Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard), E002 (Model Registry / Model Registry), E004 (Documentation / RHOAI Documentation). Assigning the registry-UI entry point (E003) to the Dashboard team is correct per model-registry.md (\"Dashboard for the registry UI\"). No epic spans multiple components or teams. No scope duplication: E002 stores/exposes lineage, E003 surfaces it in the UI — complementary halves of HLR-2, not parallel builds of the same surface; E001 and E003 share no rebuilt surface (E003 opens E001's view rather than re-implementing it). No work-product type mixing (docs isolated in E004). All epics are single-surface and well under the ~2-week guideline. The HLR-3 bundling decision is readable directly from the matrix row.\n33\t\n34\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n35\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (comparison view, lineage storage/API exposure, registry-UI entry point, published documentation). No Investigation epics — appropriate, since the strategy has no open questions and both touched components are GA with documented interfaces. No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set, and none are needed (no gates). implementation_type is null for code epics and docs-authoring for E004, as expected.\n36\t\n37\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n38\tEvery epic is type Implementation and carries `ai_signals` (the 9-signal rubric); `investigation_signals` is null everywhere, correctly. Frontmatter values match the ai-signals rationale tables in all four files. architecture_claims: 1 in each epic is corroborated by real .context files (dashboard.md and model-registry.md exist and support the cited premises: pipeline runs surfaced in the Dashboard, custom properties as the registry's extension point, registry UI rendered by the Dashboard). E004's docs-specific values (human_process_gates: -1 for the mandatory docs review process, repo_access: 0 for internal docs tooling) are well-justified. One arguable value noted as a minor issue: E002's external_dependency: 0 hedges on possible upstream schema work even though the epic's own scope pins the custom-properties approach that model-registry.md documents as the extension point — defensible conservatism, not a rubric contradiction.\n39\t\n40\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n41\tEach epic has testable Given/When/Then acceptance criteria derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs: E004 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-before-publication AC. No replacement epics exist (no rollback/feature-flag AC required), and no konflux-chain epics exist (no build-pipeline AC required). No AC states an externally-controlled precondition — every AC conditions on what its epic builds or verifies (including E001's passive-component validation AC on pipeline runs rendering). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; cross-references use descriptive phrasing (\"the Dashboard-side epic for HLR-2\").\n42\t\n43\t### 7. C